1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,077
Why do intelligent,

2
00:00:01,077 --> 00:00:04,082
experienced professionals estimate a two-week project

3
00:00:04,082 --> 00:00:06,350
timeline and routinely finish five weeks

4
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:06,690
later?

5
00:00:06,690 --> 00:00:07,704
In a 1994 study,

6
00:00:07,704 --> 00:00:09,478
psychologists Roger Buehler,

7
00:00:09,478 --> 00:00:10,302
Dale Griffin,

8
00:00:10,302 --> 00:00:12,710
and Michael Ross asked honors students

9
00:00:12,710 --> 00:00:14,674
when their theses would finish.

10
00:00:14,674 --> 00:00:17,652
Students predicted thirty-four days on average,

11
00:00:17,652 --> 00:00:20,440
yet actual completion took nearly fifty-six.

12
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:23,080
Daniel Kahneman traced this planning fallacy

13
00:00:23,080 --> 00:00:24,220
to the Inside View:

14
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:26,980
your brain pictures an uninterrupted best-case

15
00:00:26,980 --> 00:00:28,900
sprint while ignoring sick days,

16
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:29,740
scope changes,

17
00:00:29,740 --> 00:00:31,180
and hidden dependencies.

18
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:34,362
Use Kahneman's Reference Class Forecasting instead:

19
00:00:34,362 --> 00:00:36,234
check how long your last three

20
00:00:36,234 --> 00:00:38,792
similar projects actually took from start

21
00:00:38,792 --> 00:00:39,416
to finish,

22
00:00:39,416 --> 00:00:41,974
then multiply your optimistic estimate by

23
00:00:41,974 --> 00:00:42,910
one point five.

24
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:45,587
Price real-world friction into your calendar

25
00:00:45,587 --> 00:00:47,716
upfront so you hit deadlines calmly

26
00:00:47,716 --> 00:00:49,420
and protect your reputation.
